feat(deploy): the operator's side of CA custody, and the gate for it (#238)

The store starts sealed and there is no unseal key in this repository, so there
is an operator step and it cannot be automated away. #238: "A dev-mode store
that unseals with a known key is theatre and must not be what ships." A store
that unseals itself holds a key something on the machine can read, which is the
property this rung exists to remove.

The cost is stated rather than hidden: losing the unseal key loses the CA
exactly as losing a key file would. That is not a regression from rungs 1 and 2
— it is the same loss with a different custodian, and the custodian is now a
person instead of a directory anything on the box can read.

The CA is never handed the root token. `token` mints a short-lived one scoped
to signing with the CA key and reading its public half, because a CA holding the
root token could change its own permissions, which is the opposite of a custody
boundary. The unseal key is read from the terminal with echo off and is never an
argument: an argument is visible in a process listing to everything else on the
machine.

The selftest asserts what a pull request can break without anyone noticing: the
default still uses the file CA and needs no store, the overlay leaves no fileca
flag, and the CA does not mount the volume that holds ca.key. Its first case is
the control — the overlay must actually change the CA — because an overlay that
silently failed to apply would pass every "no key material" check by having
changed nothing.

MUTATION TESTING FOUND A FALSE PASS IN THIS FILE, which is the reason to trust
the rest of it. The dev-mode rule was written for the shell spelling
`server -dev` and compose writes exec form, ["server", "-dev"] — so a real
dev-mode service passed the check. Both spellings are matched now. The rule also
skips comment lines, for the reason the exemptions gate does: the overlay's own
header explains at length that there is no dev mode, and a check that fired on
the explanation would make the rule unmentionable.

